00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.123
So to get us started,

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.720
most of us are brought up
to believe that a meteor hit

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.870
the earth, leading to the
extinction of the dinosaurs,

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.640
but as this talk title suggests,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.193
it seems that it might
not be quite so simple.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.150
What is missing from this widely held idea

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:15.353
and how do fungi fit in?

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.177
- So Kate, thank you for your question

00:02:19.177 --> 00:02:20.040
and thank you for inviting me.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.040
I think the general explanation
that a meteor did it,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.140
is the right explanation.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:31.710
I mean, but there are nuances to this.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.610
The event is thought to have
been a collision from something

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:38.800
about 17 miles in length
that hit the Yucatan

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.890
65 million years ago.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:45.160
And it would've caused a
tremendous calamity in the earth,

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:49.720
massive tsunamis going
through North America,

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.550
rapid warming of the atmosphere,
massive amount of dust,

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.610
and then fires on the earth.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.720
So anything at the top of
the food chain would've been

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.643
in trouble, dinosaurs were
the top of the food chain,

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.550
but the, and probably
perhaps some survived.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:12.070
But the question is what
happened in the aftermath

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:12.903
of the calamity,

00:03:12.903 --> 00:03:15.570
what happened in the month
and the years after that?

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.380
And what hopefully we'll talk about is,

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.580
is a world dominated by the fungi

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.320
and anything that survives,

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.630
anything that made it then,

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:29.040
would've had to deal with
a massive fungal bloom

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.883
as the planet became a global compost.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.810
- So I think maybe jumping
in on that, you know,

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:40.590
what then precisely were the
sort of conditions of the,

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:44.827
this post meteor world and
how did they specifically kind

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.073
of favor fungal growth?

00:03:49.567 --> 00:03:51.823
- So there has been a lot of work

00:03:51.823 --> 00:03:53.193
reconstructing the calamity.

00:03:54.309 --> 00:03:59.130
You can imagine that the
immediate blast would would've led

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:02.520
to rapid heating of the atmosphere.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.240
And then these shock waves
would have basically went

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.253
around the world.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.342
And two things will happen then.

00:04:12.342 --> 00:04:14.800
The tsunamis, the shock waves,

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.740
will have knocked down the trees,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:21.610
but the what really hurts the
plant life is large numbers,

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:25.010
large, millions of tons of earth

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:27.760
are basically kicked into low orbit.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.130
And these now will obscure the sun,

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.210
shutting down for photosynthesis.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.200
Now, many of those rocks
are in unstable orbits

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.710
and will begin to come down

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.310
and they come down as flaming meteorites.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.010
So you have fires,

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:44.480
you have the death of vegetation,

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.130
and you have rapid cooling,

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.050
the earth cools very rapidly after that.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.341
So you have dark,

00:04:52.341 --> 00:04:54.530
dead vegetation and cool.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:59.530
And those are the ideal
conditions for a fungal bloom.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.200
- So where does then the link

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.100
with dinosaur extinctions come in?

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:09.740
So given the sort of
conditions that are favored now

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:13.090
in this really terrible
sounding, you know,

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:15.760
apocalyptic world, how,

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.760
why are fungi then favoring
infection in dinosaurs

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:22.790
at this stage then?

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.180
- So what, the way I look at it

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:30.070
is that clearly things survive.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.460
We are here.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:35.023
So clearly our early on ancestors made it,

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.180
clearly, our reptilians made it.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.140
We have reptiles today.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.450
We got iguana.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:45.190
Insects made it, dinosaurs didn't make it,

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:46.023
but you know,

00:05:46.023 --> 00:05:48.840
dinosaurs were in all kinds
of shape from enormous

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.650
to smaller ones.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:55.650
And one potential explanation for this is

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:01.490
that certainly mammals with their internal

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.683
combustion engines, they remain warm.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.450
The others, certainly
the reptiles, didn't.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.380
What happens when reptiles
are cold, they can't move,

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:12.693
they can't digest food.

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:17.590
So these conditions would've given

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.660
a huge advantage already
to anything that was warm.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.360
And now you could add the fungal spores.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.960
So in this cataclysmic
post apocalyptic war,

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.770
you would've had massive
amounts of fungal spores and it,

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:35.960
you can always overcome

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.830
an immune system with
insufficient inoculum.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:42.420
So for the cold blooded animals,

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.710
they would've been at
a great disadvantage,

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:47.800
not only nutritionally,
not only moving around,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:49.480
but now having to deal
with massive numbers

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:50.313
of fungal spores.

00:06:50.313 --> 00:06:54.270
In the meantime, our small
ancestors would've done well,

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.570
they could move, they could eat mushrooms,

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:58.970
they could eat all kinds of things,

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:02.720
and they would establish
much larger populations.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.550
So when the skies cleared and
the earth begins to recover,

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:10.200
now you have a lot more
mamas than you have reptiles,

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.970
large founder populations,

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.223
and then you have the ancient mammals.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:20.220
- And what, do we have any real,

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:22.070
do we have like concrete evidence from

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:23.260
the fossil record for this?

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:25.500
How do we, you talked about, you know,

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.900
huge blooms of spores in this time.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.030
Do we know that that happened?

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.500
- Yes, there is fungo.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:35.380
There is in the fossil
record, what is known,

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:36.620
the KT boundary.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.000
That is the Cretaceous tertiary boundary.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.660
There is evidence for
massive fungal proliferation,

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:45.170
not only there, but if
you go back in time,

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:47.030
this happened 65 million years ago.

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:50.053
If you go back to the Permian extinction,

00:07:50.920 --> 00:07:55.300
there, the fungal layer in
some parts of South Africa

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:58.520
is as much as one meter, think about that.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:01.060
one meter of fungal spores.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:03.300
So anything that survives ,

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.510
the fungi will always do
well when things go bad

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.440
for other organisms, okay.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:11.330
They, and they do very
well because they tend

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:16.330
to degrade matter and they will
survive in this environment.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:17.920
And they, when they survive,

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:19.480
they produce larger spores,

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.920
and large amounts of
spores means that you have

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:24.900
to make it through this fungal bloom.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.400
So yes, there is fossil evidence

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:31.100
for massive fungal proliferation in

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.905
those two great calamities, the Permeant

00:08:33.905 --> 00:08:36.173
and the KT boundary.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:39.720
- And so it seems like,

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.600
temperature really then
is the big player here

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:47.600
that warm blooded mamas sort
of had the advantage here

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:51.780
to protect themselves
against fungal infection.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:56.060
So how does that, how does
that sort of play out,

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.080
in the world today?

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.470
And if we're thinking about, I don't know,

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:04.140
a world of changing
climate and temperature,

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:08.980
if, how will this potentially
impact what we see

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:09.930
with fungi today

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.733
and susceptibility of
different kinds of animals?

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:15.900
- That's a great question, Rebecca.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:20.040
The, I mean, the way I came into this was

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.740
because it always bothered me,

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:23.860
the question, why mammals,

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.000
I already had three meals today.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:27.590
You know, if I was reptilian,

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:30.110
I can probably do with one meal a week.

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:32.253
I mean, how do we end up having,

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:37.350
selecting large animals that
have to eat all the time?

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.030
That doesn't make any sense to me,

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:42.240
unless there was a selection mechanism,

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:47.240
something had to favor being hot and then,

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:50.800
so this, so what I, the way

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:53.950
I think about it is that
this temperature creates

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.310
a thermal gradient
between us on the outside.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:00.930
Now it turns out that we looked
at a susceptibility of fungi

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:03.500
to temperature, about 10 years ago,

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:07.800
and most fungal species
cannot tolerate 37 degrees.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:09.750
They simply stop growing.

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:12.180
So just being 37 degrees is giving you

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.930
a tremendous protection
against the fungal kingdom,

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:16.763
which is by the way,

00:10:16.763 --> 00:10:19.160
the major passages of plants,

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:22.297
a cold blooded animal,
insects, and many others.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:25.710
So guess what's happening
in the world today?

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.990
The world is getting warmer.

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:30.320
So it, what is this going to do?

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.950
Well, one, you have a lot
of organisms out there

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:38.950
that maybe the temperature
max is 34, 35, 36 degrees,

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.080
but they are loaded with the capacity

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:43.130
to cause disease in other organisms,

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:45.900
but they can't break through
your temperature barrier,

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:47.933
but what would happen if they adapted?

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:52.140
Then as a result of a warmer climate,

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:56.460
then we can be post, we
can be facing new threats

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.010
from the, we don't even know yet

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.373
because they haven't
been a problem up to now.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.710
- And so, and I guess, adding on to

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:09.210
the kind of temperature
question here, then where do,

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:11.220
where do other animals fit in?

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.530
I think if my basic biology is correct,

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:19.530
I think birds are even
warmer than us humans.

00:11:20.136 --> 00:11:23.150
So, are they, are birds going
to take over the world next?

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:26.760
Is that going to be kind of
our trajectory from here?

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:28.280
- Well they, it's interesting you,

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:29.610
I'm glad you bring in the birds.

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:32.450
So the birds and the mammals are the two.

00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:34.056
And by the way, the crocodiles,

00:11:34.056 --> 00:11:35.987
I don't know what's going
on with the crocodiles,

00:11:35.987 --> 00:11:36.820
but the birds,

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:38.210
the mammals, and the crocodiles are

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:41.976
the major groups that make it through the,

00:11:41.976 --> 00:11:43.800
do well after the KT calamity,

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.040
the birds are already pretty high.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:49.470
The birds are quite resistant
to fungal diseases except

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.820
for one kind of fungus type,

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.750
which are already thermally adapted.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:56.450
And that's the Aspergillus,

00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:59.020
the Aspergillus family is often what you

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:00.160
find in a compost.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:01.110
You know, you drive by,

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:03.470
you see a compost, you
see smoke coming out.

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.050
Those are fungi.

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:07.180
They're carrying on metabolism.

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:10.610
These are, those fungi
are already quite adapted.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:14.350
And for whatever reason,
the birds are susceptible

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:16.300
to aspergillus, but they
are pretty resistant

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:17.370
to everything else.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:18.860
You can't, for example,

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:21.675
pigeons that are associated
with cryptococcosis,

00:12:21.675 --> 00:12:24.350
but you can't give a pigeon
a cryptococcal infection

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.290
because their temperature is 42.

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:28.870
So they are able to do so.

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:31.740
So the birds, why are the birds hot?

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:35.840
Probably because flight
requires a tremendous investment

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:37.410
in energy and the hotter the energy,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.060
the more efficient it is.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:40.700
So it turns out that

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:42.640
in the case of birds,

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.210
in the case of the primitive mammals

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:45.290
that were around,

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:48.533
they were pre adapted perhaps
for different conditions.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:52.680
- Very interesting, and so maybe

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:54.400
to kind of circle back

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:55.990
to Kate's original question,

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:59.240
then, how did this whole idea emerge?

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:00.590
How did you put,

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:03.648
how did this theory sort of
come together, given what,

00:13:03.648 --> 00:13:05.640
you know, we currently know
about fungi, but of course,

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:07.440
without being able to actually have

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.000
a record of what would've happened during

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.503
the time of the dinosaurs?

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:17.830
- Well, I mean, I like
to read outside my field

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:19.730
and I pride myself that every day I try

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:21.830
to read something about
my, outside my field,

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:26.830
and the astro hypotheses came
to be in the early eighties

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:31.660
when it was, when an
iridium level was found.

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:34.060
And that's what give the clue
that it came from outer space,

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:35.660
between, in the KT the boundary.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:40.400
But, I always been bothered
by the question, why mammals?

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:43.976
Now people say, well, you
know, you had a calamity

00:13:43.976 --> 00:13:47.910
and it created a space
and the mammals moved in

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.120
because the dinosaurs are not there.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:52.000
That doesn't make any sense to me.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:55.720
You know, you still have reptiles,
you'll still have others.

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:57.840
Why do you end up selecting something

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.233
that needs so much food?

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:03.210
You needed to have something
at the end of the day,

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:07.060
that for which this temperature
provided an advantage,

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:10.470
otherwise we make no sense
and that separated the birds.

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:12.720
The birds already had that
advantage in the sense

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:13.770
that they need it.

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:15.370
They may have had it for flight,

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:20.420
but for the terrestrial
one of us, the creatures.

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:22.810
And so, that always bothered me.

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:24.820
It never made any sense to
me that people said, well,

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:27.790
a space got created and
the mammals took over now.

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:28.940
You need to have something.

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:29.840
And then what happened,

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:33.250
reading out, reading, trying
to read in other areas,

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:37.550
I came to that article showing in 2003,

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:40.870
over a fungal bloom
around the KT boundary,

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:44.630
that was published, I
believe it was in Science.

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:45.931
And the two things began to make,

00:14:45.931 --> 00:14:50.580
began to fit in together, fungal bloom,

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:55.580
thermal protection, mammalian dominance.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.703
So it's really a thought,

00:14:58.955 --> 00:15:03.090
it's this idea comes from linking
in very different aspects,

00:15:04.136 --> 00:15:06.773
facts into new thought.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.160
- And is there anything that we can then

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:15.700
take from this and sort of apply?

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:19.070
If we're thinking about how
to protect either ourselves

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:24.020
as humans or other animals
against fungal infections,

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:26.510
can we exploit this idea of temperature

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:29.430
as being sort of protective?

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:31.810
Or is there somewhere
we can sort of take this

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:33.610
and learn from this?

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:36.690
- I think that, yes, I think
that there's a lot to do here,

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:39.930
but maybe first we can visit the bats.

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:42.470
So it turns out that the
bats in North America

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:45.400
were doing pretty well up to 2006.

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:48.890
In 2006, a fungus was
introduced from Europe

00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:50.410
that is a cold loving fungus.

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:53.230
So it can only grow in cold temperatures

00:15:53.230 --> 00:15:55.220
and began to kill the bats.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:57.410
It began in New York and in New York state

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:59.950
and it spread through
a whole new continent

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:01.930
and the bats are susceptible in

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:04.950
the winter, not in the summer.

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:08.470
So the bats, the reason is,
in the temperate climates,

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:09.303
there is no food.

00:16:09.303 --> 00:16:11.410
So the bats hibernate,

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:14.550
the fungus grows on them and
essentially wakes them up

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:15.540
in the middle of the winter

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:18.170
because they're sick and
they can't feed and they die.

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:22.730
If you take those bats from
the cave to the laboratory

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:26.070
and feed them, just giving them food,

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:27.980
drives up their metabolic rate,

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:29.880
and they can clear the fungal disease.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:33.233
That's giving you a tremendous,

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:39.680
powerful anecdote of the
power of temperature.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.490
Another example are the
frogs that are infected

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:43.950
with the Chytrid fungus.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:47.100
If you bring them into a lab
and you put them at 37 degrees,

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:48.750
you can cure them of the Chytrid.

00:16:49.665 --> 00:16:53.890
So, it is possible.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:55.640
So there are a couple of
things you can think of,

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:58.580
you can think of that
for infectious diseases

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.710
that are acquired from the environment,

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:03.570
that we may be able to
explore temperature,

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:05.760
that perhaps we shouldn't
be giving everybody

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:10.150
Tylenol when they have
a severe fungal disease,

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:11.330
that the temperature,

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:14.130
increasing the temperature
could make the fungus

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:19.130
more susceptible to the
internal environment.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:22.880
The other thing to think about
is that temperature pathways

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:25.193
may be targeted in drug development.

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:29.960
We know very little about how do you adapt

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:31.620
to higher temperatures.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.090
This is an area that, you know,

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:35.150
almost, let me give you a thought example,

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:37.070
almost everything we know in immunology

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:39.170
is done at 37 degrees,

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:42.650
but immunology in your
skin happens at 31 degrees.

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:46.520
You would think that there has to be

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:50.660
a lot of more research
looking at a temperature

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:54.260
as a variable in all aspects
of micro and pathogenesis.

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:55.830
But yes, I think that,

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:59.653
that it is something that
we currently don't target.

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:03.192
So it is a target to me,

00:18:03.192 --> 00:18:06.100
it's a target for exploration
of possible opportunity.

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:11.090
- Very cool, and,

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:15.380
so it sort of centering
this around reptiles

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:21.590
and the dinosaurs and the
sort of advantage of mammals,

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:27.470
but to today, we know that
certainly humans and other,

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:31.730
I think other mamas are
still to a extent susceptible

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:33.750
to fungal disease.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:37.500
So is this just sort of
the exception to the rule

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:40.130
or where does sort of human vulnerability

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:42.173
to fungal infection then fit in?

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:47.500
- So I think that the
fungal diseases emerge

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:49.550
in the 20th century as a major,

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:51.400
we know tuberculosis from antiquity.

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:54.060
We know a lot of diseases
from a long time,

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:57.770
but fungal diseases really
begin to take off up to 1950.

00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:01.160
What happens in 1950,
corticosteroids are developed.

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:02.780
Chemotherapy is developed.

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:04.330
Then in the 1980s,

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:05.800
you have the HIV epidemic.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:08.120
What do all these things have in common?

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:12.420
They interfere with our immunity.

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:16.780
So there are two pillars
to our resistance.

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:19.220
One pillar is immunity,

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:22.750
advanced immunity, having
T-cells, B cells, innate immunity,

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:23.980
you name it.

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:25.750
And the other one is temperature.

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:28.150
So what we saw in the
20th century was that

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:31.100
if you mess around with
the pillar of immunity,

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:33.520
you get more fungal diseases.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:36.760
What we are afraid of to
see in the 21st century

00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:40.210
is that the fungi may
adapt and then defeat

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:41.763
or mess with our second pillar.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:48.070
But I think it is a fact that
immunologically competent

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:52.480
mammals are remarkably
resistant to fungal disease.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:54.810
They do occur, but they are very rare.

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:57.740
And what we are learning
now with genomic analysis

00:19:57.740 --> 00:19:59.810
is that when you, when they occur,

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:02.820
they often tend to be some
sort of error, for example,

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.660
a mutation in detecting
pathway or signaling

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:08.420
or something like that,

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:09.770
that sets it apart.

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:13.830
So I would focus more on
the remarkable resistance,

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:17.360
and try to understand that,
rather than the susceptibility,

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:19.660
because I think we, the susceptibility,

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:21.160
that has many causes,

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:23.750
and you can certainly pave
the the way with drugs,

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:25.380
transplants, and things like that.

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.333
To me, that's a very
interesting scientific question.

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:33.550
- Definitely, yeah, I think
in our current pandemic era,

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:35.370
I think we're, we all, you know, I think

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:38.220
everyone is very attuned
to thinking about kind

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:39.900
of human vulnerabilities

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:42.610
and immunological kind of susceptibility.

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:43.820
So it's very interesting.

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:48.820
I mean, it's certainly
kind of an out there theory

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:54.120
and is different, I think from,

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:55.970
what we are used to thinking about

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.080
and not necessarily intuitive to kind

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:03.080
of center this important
event around these kind

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:04.550
of fungal species and the impact

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:05.383
that they've had.

00:21:05.383 --> 00:21:08.340
So has there been sort of push back

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:09.415
to this theory?

00:21:09.415 --> 00:21:11.600
Are there people who think otherwise?

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:13.270
- We have a good time.

00:21:13.270 --> 00:21:15.743
Its one possibility that nobody cares.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:18.830
- That's fair.

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:22.297
- Another possibility is that
people say, well, you know,

00:21:23.461 --> 00:21:25.430
it may be.

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:27.140
And they, but I,

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:29.420
what I would say you is that
as a scientist, you know,

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:31.640
there are three big questions.

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:33.430
What, how, and why?

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:35.000
I mean, I'm on the other big questions.

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:38.650
So scientists are pretty good
at why, we describe stuff.

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:41.240
Now, people like to work on mechanism.

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:42.360
That's how you do it,

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:44.610
but we tend to stay away from the why.

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:48.703
The why gets into, often
gets people very, very,

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:52.550
worried, you get into metaphysics,

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:56.290
you get into theological
reasoning, whatever,

00:21:56.290 --> 00:21:58.000
but I am interested in all three.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.680
And to me the why questions,

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:04.300
it really often explain a
lot of the world as it is.

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:06.740
So to me, a lot of this came from,

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:09.490
I'm trying to understand the
why question, why mammals?

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:14.930
To me that's a really
interesting issue that if,

00:22:14.930 --> 00:22:18.800
that needs an explanation
and whatever was out there

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:22.673
was certainly not sufficient for me.

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:27.360
And it's been a lot of fun,
look, this is something

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.855
you can talk at a cocktail party.

00:22:29.855 --> 00:22:30.688
I mean.
- Absolutely.

00:22:30.688 --> 00:22:32.010
- I can't talk about my other work

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:34.971
at a cocktail party without
having everybody leave.

00:22:34.971 --> 00:22:35.804
- Yeah.

00:22:37.936 --> 00:22:41.620
- Maybe I'll just jump to remind
any of the audience members

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:42.820
that if they have any questions,

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:46.270
they should feel free to drop
them into the Q and A section,

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:47.113
of the webinar.

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:49.590
And while I have the microphone,

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:52.777
I might take opportunity
to circle back to some of

00:22:52.777 --> 00:22:54.390
the questions around temperature

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.693
and make a connection with climate change.

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:00.510
What do we,

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:03.320
do we think that climate
change may effect fungi.

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.690
And if so, how,

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:08.410
what about their ability
to cause infection?

00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:12.200
- So we have one example
again, it needs explanation,

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:13.600
it falls into a white question.

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:15.250
I pose it to you,

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:19.050
Candida aureus was unknown
to medicine 'till 2007.

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:21.080
You can't find it in any
collection, you cannot.

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:23.640
In 2010 and 2011,

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:27.400
it emerges simultaneously
in three continents

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:30.610
and they're not, the clays are different.

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:32.530
So these are not that somebody took it

00:23:32.530 --> 00:23:35.280
and flew from one country to
another, three continents.

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:38.030
Why would that be?

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:41.190
Why would you have this thing
emerge at the same time?

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:42.950
And then you may begin to look and say,

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:45.853
what is happening in common
on the three continents?

00:23:46.847 --> 00:23:49.670
And we have proposed that this may

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:53.700
be the first example
of an organism adapting

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:55.567
to higher temperature and breaking through

00:23:55.567 --> 00:23:56.680
the thermal barrier.

00:23:56.680 --> 00:23:58.240
Now, whether it is right or not,

00:23:58.240 --> 00:23:59.720
I don't know.

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:02.640
There, it was just recovered
in the Andaman Islands.

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:05.320
It turns out that the wild isolate,

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:07.960
it was less temperature
adapted than the ones

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:09.580
that they recovered from a beach

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:11.540
that could have been put there by humans.

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:14.920
So what I would argue is
that we need to focus,

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:16.560
why questions are fun,

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:19.830
why questions can drive
all kinds of research

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:21.380
and they can also open the areas

00:24:21.380 --> 00:24:25.133
that we don't know that
could also be addressed.

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:28.430
- Hmm, good thing we have a program

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:30.880
called fungal kingdom,
threats and opportunities.

00:24:32.639 --> 00:24:33.839
- Well, I'm glad you do.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:37.120
- So I'll read another question

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:39.000
that's come in from the audience.

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:41.780
And they, they ask if you
have any comments on how long

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:44.850
the dinosaur extinction
event would have taken,

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:47.220
how abroad was the fungal bloom,

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:49.210
and how long would a fungal infection take

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:50.473
to kill a dinosaur?

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:55.210
- So I guess I begin by
saying, I don't know,

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:57.210
but, and then once you say, I don't know,

00:24:57.210 --> 00:25:00.220
I think we can say things
like, well, there is evidence.

00:25:00.220 --> 00:25:02.476
People have found evidence that they may,

00:25:02.476 --> 00:25:05.400
still may have survived the initial blast.

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:09.220
It's really not clear
whether they all died

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:10.900
in around a few weeks

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:13.580
of that horrible day,
65 million years ago,

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:15.950
or whether in some parts
of earth, some survived,

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:17.750
then subsequently went extinct,

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:21.660
I think the fungal bloom
would've taken weeks to months

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:24.340
to years, you need to have vegetation die.

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:26.720
And we know what happens in the forest.

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:29.600
It takes a while for
things to begin to decay.

00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:34.600
And, but it's probably, we
would be dealing with decades,

00:25:34.790 --> 00:25:39.790
if not hundreds of years of
massive numbers of fungal spores

00:25:40.540 --> 00:25:43.997
and whatever survived
had to deal with that.

00:25:43.997 --> 00:25:46.960
- Well, so a related
question that has come in

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:51.340
is asking whether when
species became extinct

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.910
might be directly linked
to body temperature?

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:56.970
So the question is
related to the sauropods

00:25:56.970 --> 00:25:59.810
that have 37 degrees
Celsius body temperature,

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:01.390
which became the dominant species

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:02.840
in the early Jurassic period.

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:05.040
- Yeah, so that,

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:07.863
but people said, the way
to preface that question is

00:26:07.863 --> 00:26:09.980
how do, there is some evidence,

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:13.160
there is actually some evidence
dinosaurs were warm blooded.

00:26:13.160 --> 00:26:14.140
How does this fit?

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:16.940
Well, notice what I'm saying. I'm saying

00:26:17.813 --> 00:26:20.990
that the great killing of
this group of organisms

00:26:20.990 --> 00:26:22.203
was the calamity.

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:24.643
There is, even though they,

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:28.960
the idea of high temperatures
in them may come from

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:32.470
the fact they are enormous
masses and they're anything.

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:34.470
And just having all that cytoplasm,

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:36.710
that double likely would
drive up the temperature.

00:26:36.710 --> 00:26:39.060
But I don't think we have any
evidence that they regulated

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:40.830
the temperature like we do,

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:42.880
or that they could've been
able to maintain those

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:47.350
temperatures when, when in a situation

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:51.043
of dropping global
temperatures, lack of food.

00:26:52.016 --> 00:26:55.940
So I think that it's not a
problem for this hypothesis

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:59.660
because the postulation
is those large ones,

00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:01.420
probably were the top of the food chain

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:04.500
and that would've been in
deep trouble in the calamity,

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:06.100
whether they were right near it

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:08.590
or on the other side of the earth.

00:27:08.590 --> 00:27:09.423
- We're almost out of a time.

00:27:09.423 --> 00:27:12.360
So maybe I'll close
with one last question,

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:15.350
but it, the question is if fungi have,

00:27:15.350 --> 00:27:19.101
haven't traditionally co-evolved
to be human pathogens,

00:27:19.101 --> 00:27:20.760
will increased tolerance
to higher temperatures

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:21.593
by climate change

00:27:21.593 --> 00:27:25.173
be enough to make them
serious human pathogens?

00:27:26.460 --> 00:27:30.100
- So what, people often say, you know, or,

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:31.720
becoming a pathogen is a good thing.

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:33.790
What is the data for that?

00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:37.110
In fact, I would argue that
most microbes pay a price

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:39.490
in getting the capacity for virulence,

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:40.450
what does it give you?

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:42.610
Well, it gives you a new place to grow,

00:27:42.610 --> 00:27:44.340
and it may give you transportation.

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:45.730
If you're getting to an animal.

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:47.240
They can be moved around.

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:50.060
But when we actually wrote
an article about the cost

00:27:50.060 --> 00:27:52.680
of virulence, so when people say, "Well,

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:54.150
why haven't the fungi adopted?"

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:55.540
Well, you know what,

00:27:55.540 --> 00:27:57.890
this may not necessarily
be in their best interest,

00:27:57.890 --> 00:28:00.850
or there may have be no
selection pressure for it.

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:05.160
But I think that if we get
more, it will be by accident,

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:09.120
you will basically end up
raising the temperature,

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:12.760
the thermal temperature
in which they can grow,

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:14.680
because they have to
live in a warmer world.

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:18.040
And some of them will be
loaded with the characteristics

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:19.360
that will allow it to cause disease.

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:21.930
There is no intelligence behind this.

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:24.513
There is just evolution and selection.

00:28:26.275 --> 00:28:28.510
- Well, thank you both so much for sort

00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:30.690
of a whirlwind tour and a fascinating talk

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:33.230
on what is a fascinating hypothesis.

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:35.597
So thank you so much to Arturo Casadevall,

00:28:35.597 --> 00:28:37.670
and to Rebecca Shapiro for sharing

00:28:37.670 --> 00:28:40.020
your insights with us today.

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:43.150
And thank you to all the
audience members for joining in.

00:28:43.150 --> 00:28:45.580
We'll have a recording
of this talk available

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:48.280
on our website, CIFAR.CA,

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:50.350
likely sometime early next week.

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:51.539
So thank you all.

00:28:51.539 --> 00:28:52.527
- Thank you for having me.

00:28:52.527 --> 00:28:55.320
It's been a lot of fun,
science is a lot of fun.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:56.761
- That's right thanks.

00:28:56.761 --> 00:28:57.919
(Arturo mumbling)

00:28:57.919 --> 00:29:01.209
- Wonderful, thank you.
- Thank you.

00:29:01.209 --> 00:29:03.876
